\caption(The encoder and bounded-prefix decoder return identical actual field boundaries. Five bits plus one value entry and one word record give seven logical capture entries.)
\alttext(Five native bit cells show a blue zero, green one-one and orange one-zero. Their indices zero through four and field labels expose the order-two word for ten.)

> Derive the code and its inverse

For a known order k>=0, write n=q·2^k+r, with 0<=r<2^k.
Let m=floor(log2(q+1)) mathematically. Emit m zeros, binary(q+1)
in m+1 bits, then r in exactly k bits, most significant bit first:

\[
L_k(n)=m+(m+1)+k=2\lfloor\log_2(\lfloor n/2^k\rfloor+1)\rfloor+1+k.
\]

After seeing m zeros and the first one, the decoder knows that m+k
further bits complete this word. If their combined unsigned value is z,
then n=(2^m−1)·2^k+z. Equivalently, recover the quotient from the m
bits after the one, then attach the low k bits. This is the order-k
inverse in ITU-T H.274 section 9.1, equation (72).

For fixed k, two different zero-run lengths first differ where one word
has reached its leading one and the other still has zero. Equal zero-run
lengths imply equal total word lengths. Hence no distinct word is a
prefix of another. Each fixed-length field is preserved exactly, so
concatenated complete words have a unique inverse. The outer message
still needs a stopping rule: prefix-free words alone do not identify
where one message ends and another begins.

The mathematical construction admits arbitrarily large integers. This API
accepts every uint64 value and orders 0 through 63. It avoids forming
q+1 when that would equal 2^64, and never shifts a uint64 by 64.
Order zero is gamma coding of n+1, so zero is valid and encodes as 1.
This differs from positive-integer gamma coding of n itself.

A vector<bool> stores the logical bit sequence using its specialised
container representation. The API defines neither a packed-byte format
nor byte endianness. exp_golomb_bit_string produces ASCII debug text;
its one-character-per-bit storage is different again. All comparisons
below count payload bits, excluding order, count, width and other framing.

> Preserve the original examples and find the exact threshold

The original report lists zero through eight and round-trips
{0,1,3,7,15,100,1000,65535}. These codewords remain unchanged.
Its eight values occupy 90 order-zero bits against 128 bits for a
16-bit common field. Here 16 is also the smallest common width,
because 65535 occurs in the sample.

Length is a staircase in n, including on a log2(n+1) axis. It is constant
for 2^m−1<=n<=2^(m+1)−2 and increases by two at each threshold.
L_0(n)<16 exactly when 0<=n<=254; at 255 the length jumps to 17.
The native length helper obtains this from integer widths without
encoding an unnecessary temporary word.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/exp_golomb_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| n | original order-zero word | bits |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(std::uint64_t n=0;n<=8;++n)std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",n,dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_bit_string(dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_encode({n})),dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_code_length(n));
  const std::vector<std::uint64_t> values{0,1,3,7,15,100,1000,65535};
  const auto e=dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_encode_trace(values);const auto d=dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_decode_work(e.bits,int(values.size()));const auto s=dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_size(values);
  if(d.values!=values||s.bits!=90||s.fixed_bits!=128||d.consumed_bits!=e.bits.size())throw std::runtime_error("original sample failed");
  std::print("\nOriginal mixed sequence: {} values; {} coded bits; fixed width {}; {} fixed bits; actual capture {}; round trip {}; fully consumed {}.\n",s.values,s.bits,s.fixed_width,s.fixed_bits,e.captured_entries,d.values==values,d.consumed_bits==e.bits.size());
  std::print("\n| original sampled n | exact length | declared fixed bits |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::uint64_t n:std::vector<std::uint64_t>{0,1,3,7,15,63,255,1023,4095,65535})std::print("| {} | {} | 16 |\n",n,dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_code_length(n));
  std::size_t shorter=0;std::vector<double>x,lengths,fixed;
  for(std::uint64_t n=0;n<=1023;++n){if(dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_code_length(n)<16)++shorter;x.push_back(n);lengths.push_back(dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_code_length(n));fixed.push_back(16);}
  std::print("\nExact comparison over 0..1023: {} values use fewer than 16 bits; length(254) {}; length(255) {}.\n",shorter,dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_code_length(254),dm::algorithms::exp_golomb_code_length(255));
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.line(x,lengths,"Order zero",{37,99,235}).line(x,fixed,"Fixed 16 bits",{220,38,38})
    .title("Discrete code lengths: the jump at 255").x_label("Value n").y_label("Payload bits per value").y_limits(0,23).size(1000,530).save_svg("build/exp_golomb_original.svg");
}
